[THOUGHT] The case presents a highly suggestive clinical triad: (1) multiple atypical duodenal ulcers indicating pathological acid hypersecretion, (2) severe hypercalcemia (Ca²⁺ = 13.2 mg/dL), and (3) absence of common alternative causes (e.g., malignancy, granulomatous disease, thiazide use, vitamin D intoxication — none reported). While MEN1 is the *most likely underlying syndrome*, the diagnosis of MEN1 itself is **not yet confirmed** — it requires either:  
 ✓ Documentation of ≥2 of the three primary endocrine tumors (parathyroid adenoma/hyperplasia, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor [e.g., gastrinoma], pituitary adenoma), **or**  
 ✓ Identification of a pathogenic germline *MEN1* variant.  
Currently, only *circumstantial evidence* supports MEN1: hypercalcemia strongly implies primary hyperparathyroidism (1st component), and multiple duodenal ulcers + high suspicion for gastrinoma imply a functional pancreatic NET (2nd component). However:  
 – Fasting gastrin has **not yet been reported**, so gastrinoma remains *presumed*, not confirmed;  
 – iPTH is **not yet available**, so primary hyperparathyroidism is *inferred*, not verified (e.g., could be tertiary or lithium-induced, though unlikely);  
 – No imaging (e.g., EUS, somatostatin receptor PET/CT) or chromogranin A to localize or corroborate neuroendocrine tumor;  
 – No pituitary evaluation (prolactin, IGF-1) to assess for third component — though not required for diagnosis, its absence leaves the syndromic diagnosis incomplete.  
Critically, **turns are at 15/16** — one turn remains. While urgent PPI initiation and calcium management are appropriate, the *diagnostic confirmation* of MEN1 hinges on objective biochemical (iPTH, gastrin) and/or genetic evidence — none yet provided. Without at least one confirmed component beyond inference, labeling the diagnosis as “MEN1” is premature.

[DECISION] INCOMPLETE

[FEEDBACK] Required before finalizing MEN1 diagnosis:  
 • **Intact parathyroid hormone (iPTH)** to confirm primary hyperparathyroidism (must be elevated or inappropriately normal with hypercalcemia);  
 • **Fasting serum gastrin level**, ideally with gastric pH <2 (to exclude achlorhydria) — a level >1000 pg/mL strongly supports gastrinoma;  
 • If gastrin is markedly elevated and iPTH confirms primary hyperparathyroidism, then MEN1 is *biochemically supported* — genetic testing would then be confirmatory but not strictly required for clinical diagnosis. These two labs are essential and feasible within the final turn.

